Josh Rambo wrote:
> Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> 
>> Hadn't there once been a more "verbose" acronym form of
>>
>>   /WoDeath
>>
>> which would lead to the plural
>>
>>   /WsoDeath
> 
> That isn't "verbose", that's redundant.

It is not verbose but it is "verbose", as I wrote. (Mind the quotes[*].)
And, yes, it is redundant. (Without quotes.)

[*] Quotes are *not* redundant; they carry necessary information!

And if you like nitpicking about redundancy; /oDeath is also redundant
(even /De is, in NH's context, from an information theory perspective).

> Why include the ascii symbol if 
> you're going to use the W to signify that it's a wand?

The question was pluralisation so pluralizing an acronym letter might
be more straigtforward than putting an s behind a graphical character.
Personally I don't see any need of introducing a plural s (or whatever
else) behind, or worse, within an acronym; rather I find it distracting.
Introducing an acronym is like introducing a new term, somthing like
a new "noun", that IMO should get its own plural ending. Though NH's
"geek abbreviations" are wor^H^H^H another thing; more often than not
they reduce the speed of reading and increase the time necessary to
comprehend the text.

AFAIR, first there was WoD, then came WoDeath do resolve ambiguity
(since /oD is also ambiguous[**]), and then there was the redundant
/WoDeath introduced, because HoB is yet less comprehensible than
the [HoB, and now some removed the "redundancy". (Now with quotes!)
(You may take the acronym list in Dylan's spoiler files as standard,
http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/abbr-343.txt, but even there
the ambiguities are not resolved - but mentioned.)

"Th rsn why I wrt t wth rdndncy s tht n cmmnctns y nd lt f rdndncy t
mk t pssbl t ndrstnd txt sly nd fst." Or in another representation:
"The reason why I write it with redundancy is that in communications
you need lot of redundancy to make it possible to understand a text
easily and fast." (And the first of the two texts has still a _lot_
of redundancy.) It's a question of "Quality of Service"!

[**] When the abbreviations were introduced many people were not even
aware that GDSM or WoD is ambiguous.

